<p class='text'>Does it ever stop?</p>
<p class='text'>Every time it seems that I have reached rock bottom, something else comes along to kick me in the teeth.  It makes me think of two things.  One is the classic saying "waiting for the other shoe to drop" and the other is the line from Springsteen's song "Seeds": "How many times can you get up after you've been hit?"  I'm starting to wonder about that one non-rhetorically.</p>
<p class='text'>It all "started" back in March.  Well, actually, I'm sure it started before then, but I found out about it in March.  Even that isn't true, because we'd known back in February that she was deliberately lying to us and involving others in keeping secrets from us.  But it is was in March that, in the course of another conversation, we called her out on it.  We had a stand-up row on Grove St in Wilmslow where I was "forced" into admitting that I didn't think our relationship was "normal".  I don't want to get into all the details here, but she followed up later by saying that "we didn't treat her like an adult" and burble, burble.  When we tried to patch things up and/or gloss over it the following weekend, she just laid into us again, saying that we were "ignoring the issues".</p>
<p class='text'>Yeah, so I fell into that trap as well, and sent her a long email outlining what the issues were as I saw them and suggesting all the ways we could proceed.  Inasmuch as she ever responded - one line in an email six weeks later - she indicated that she did not feel it was worth answering because none of the options I had outlined were acceptable.</p>
<p class='text'>Since then, it has become increasingly clear that any option - except for her to continue in denial about the whole thing, get all of our friends and family to enter into a conspiracy of silence, and generally ignore us - was not acceptable.  And the conclusion at the end of the day is that she has effectively cut us off; but has not admitted as much.</p>
<p class='text'>The really galling thing is that it seems that people just choose to believe the really quite preposterous lies that she tells about us - that we are a danger to her children, for example, or that my wife is bipolar - in spite of having known us for years.  How the !%%! is that even <i>possible</i>?  Some of this may be on me, but it leaves me nowhere to go but to have nothing more to do with them - fully 50% of my social circle - because I cannot deal with people who will say one thing to my face and then turn around and do something completely different behind my back.</p>
<p class='text'>I'm reduced to a quivering wreck.  I'm trying to get on with my life, to write some code, to contemplate writing some of my book, but nothing "works".  It's hard to describe if you haven't been here.  It's probably some version of depression.  I need to escape.</p>
<p class='text'>And it's been nine months since I last saw my grandchildren.</p>
